ws_reconnect_jitter.s:
src/lib/ws.ts, in `function retry()`:

  const delay = Math.min(30_000, 500 * 2 ** tries++);

Every disconnected client retries at exactly the same millisecond: 500, 1000, 2000, 4000... A mass disconnect — a deploy, a Cloudflare incident, a ws-worker restart — produces a perfectly synchronised thundering herd that re-kills the service on each cycle and never converges.

Replace with half-jitter:

  const base = Math.min(30_000, 500 * 2 ** tries++);
  const delay = base / 2 + Math.random() * (base / 2);

WHY HALF-JITTER AND NOT FULL: full jitter (`Math.random() * base`) is the textbook AWS recommendation and spreads better, but it drops the effective floor to ~0ms, which makes a single transient drop reconnect almost instantly and can hammer a briefly-degraded worker. Half-jitter keeps a guaranteed minimum wait AND spreads the herd across a window. Do not use `delay + Math.random() * 1000` — a fixed-width jitter stops spreading once base exceeds it, which is exactly the regime (base = 30s) where the herd is largest.

TESTS FIRST: src/lib/__tests__/ws.test.ts. Use `vi.useFakeTimers()` and stub `Math.random`. Assert that with `Math.random` -> 0 the scheduled delay is `base / 2`, with -> 0.999 it is just under `base`, that the two differ, and that both land inside `[base/2, base]` for at least tries=0 and tries=6 (the clamped 30s case). Watch them fail against the current deterministic line — the two stubbed runs will produce identical delays.

hooks_skip_sv_check.s:
src/hooks.server.ts, inside `handle`:

  const sv = await hub_sv_get(env, x2_ws, s.user.id).catch(() => null);
  if (sv === null || sv <= s.v) event.locals.user = s.user;
  else event.cookies.delete('session', { path: '/' });

This runs on EVERY request — every page load, every API call, every /media/* fetch — and blocks on a service-binding fetch to that user's ChatHub Durable Object to read one integer (the session version, used by logout-all). The session cookie is already an HMAC-signed token that `decode_session` verifies locally in microseconds; this line throws that property away and adds a network round trip to one fixed DO location on the critical path of everything. A user far from that DO pays it on every request.

CHANGE: keep `decode_session` in hooks (signature + expiry verification stay). Move the `sv` comparison to the places that actually need it:
- src/routes/api/wstoken/+server.ts, before minting the websocket token
- src/routes/api/profile/+server.ts
- src/routes/api/auth/set-password/+server.ts
- src/routes/api/logout-all/+server.ts

Add one helper so the check lives in exactly one place — in src/lib/server/session.ts:

  export async function session_current(env: QEnv, ws: Fetcher, uid: string, v: number): Promise<boolean> {
    const sv = await hub_sv_get(env, ws, uid).catch(() => null);
    return sv === null || sv <= v;
  }

Note `sv === null` means the hub was unreachable and must FAIL OPEN, matching the current behaviour — a dead ws worker must not log everyone out. Preserve that exactly.

TRADE-OFF, state it in the commit message: a session revoked via logout-all now survives on already-issued cookies until the websocket reconnects or the 7-day expiry, on read-only routes only. Writes and socket opens are still checked. If instant global revocation is required instead, the correct fix is a 15-minute cookie refreshed on the ws handshake — do NOT reintroduce a per-request DO call.

TESTS FIRST: src/__tests__/hooks.server.test.ts currently asserts hub_sv_get IS called; invert it — assert it is NOT called for an ordinary authenticated request, and that locals.user is still populated. Then add cases in src/routes/api/wstoken/__tests__/server.test.ts: a current `v` mints a token, a stale `v` (sv > v) is rejected, and an unreachable hub still mints (fail open). Watch the inverted hooks test fail first (hub_sv_get will have been called).

hub_watch_fix.s:
Two coupled bugs. Fixing (a) without (b) in the SAME change creates an outage, so they are one step.

(a) INVERSION. src/routes/app/chat/[id]/+page.svelte builds `const watch = { type: 'watch', peer: data.peer };` and ws_sends it. ws/src/hub.ts `webSocketMessage` handles it as:

  } else if (msg.type === 'watch') {
      const id = this.env.CHAT_HUB.idFromName(msg.peer);
      const stub = this.env.CHAT_HUB.get(id);
      await stub.fetch('https://dummy/unwatch', { ... });

A watch request calls /unwatch. Worse, the `/watch` handler in the same file (the `url.pathname === '/watch'` branch) is UNREACHABLE: ws/src/index.ts routes only `/hub/:uid/(unread|read|mute|unmute|mutes|sub|unsub|convs|conv|sv)` and nothing else ever fetches it. Consequence: the `watchers` array is permanently `[]`, `notify_watchers` always iterates zero times, and live presence never pushes — the green dot only moves when the client sends an explicit `check`.

FIX: change the `watch` branch to fetch `https://dummy/watch`. Add a sibling `else if (msg.type === 'unwatch')` branch that fetches `https://dummy/unwatch`. In the client, the teardown path that currently calls `ws_drop(watch)` must ALSO `ws_send({ type: 'unwatch', peer: data.peer })` — ws_drop only stops the message being replayed on reconnect, it does not tell the peer's hub anything.

(b) THE STALL THIS UNCOVERS. ws/src/hub.ts `notify_watchers`:

  for (const watcher of watchers) {
      try { ... await stub.fetch('https://dummy/notify', ...); } catch {}
  }

Sequential. Today it is harmless because watchers is always empty. The moment (a) lands it runs on every connect AND every disconnect, and a user with 500 watchers pays 500 serialised DO round trips inside the input gate — blocking their own message delivery for the duration. Fix in the same commit:

  const MAX_WATCHERS = 200;  // module scope
  await Promise.all(
    watchers.slice(0, MAX_WATCHERS).map(async (watcher) => {
      try { ... } catch {}
    })
  );

and cap the list in the `/watch` handler too (`if (watchers.length >= MAX_WATCHERS) return new Response('ok');`) so storage cannot grow past the cap in the first place.

TESTS FIRST, in ws/src/__tests__/hub-do.test.ts:
- a `watch` message ADDS the sender to the target hub's watchers (currently it removes — watch this one fail)
- an `unwatch` message removes them
- connect and disconnect each notify every watcher
- with 300 watchers registered, at most 200 notify fetches are issued, and they are issued CONCURRENTLY — assert this with deferred mock promises: all stubs must have been called before the first deferred resolves. A sequential loop fails that assertion; that is the regression guard.

hub_message_reads.s:
Swap the read paths from Qdrant to the mailbox. Depends on hub_message_writes having run long enough that new messages are in both stores; historical messages arrive in migrate_messages_to_hub, so during this step expect old threads to look short until that runs. Do not deploy this to production before the migration.

(a) HISTORY. Add a `/msgs` GET handler to ws/src/hub.ts:

  const conv = url.searchParams.get('conv')!;
  const before = Number(url.searchParams.get('before')) || undefined;
  const rows = this.sql.exec(
    before === undefined
      ? `SELECT * FROM m WHERE conv = ? ORDER BY d DESC LIMIT 50`
      : `SELECT * FROM m WHERE conv = ? AND d < ? ORDER BY d DESC LIMIT 50`,
    ...(before === undefined ? [conv] : [conv, before])
  ).toArray();

Return `rows.reverse()` (ascending) to match the existing `page_msgs` contract exactly, and JSON.parse `fl` and `rx` back into objects before returning. `d < ?` (strict) matches the existing `start_from: before - 1` semantics — the boundary message must not repeat.

Route it by adding `msgs` to the alternation in ws/src/index.ts: `/^\/hub\/([^/]+)\/(unread|read|mute|unmute|mutes|sub|unsub|convs|conv|sv|msgs)$/` — and note the handler must forward `url.search`, which the current `/hub/` branch does NOT do (it builds `https://dummy/${action}` with no query string). Fix that or `conv` never arrives.

Add `hub_msgs()` to src/lib/server/hub_client.ts following the `hub_convs` shape INCLUDING the ChatHubError throw — a dead hub must surface as an error banner, never as 'no messages'. Then point src/routes/api/messages/+server.ts, src/routes/app/chat/[id]/+page.server.ts and src/routes/app/rooms/[id]/+page.server.ts at it instead of get_messages/get_group_messages.

(b) CONVERSATION LIST. ws/src/hub.ts `list_convs` currently lists `conv:` keys then does TWO awaited storage reads per entry (`is_muted`, then `unread:`) — a 2N N+1 inside the input gate, on every /app/chats load. `unread_totals` does the same and runs on EVERY push send, making it the hottest path in the file. Replace both with one mute list plus SQL:

  const mutes = await this.state.storage.list<MuteEntry>({ prefix: 'mute:' });
  const rows = this.sql.exec(
    `SELECT conv, MAX(d) AS last, COUNT(*) AS n FROM m GROUP BY conv ORDER BY last DESC`
  ).toArray();

Derive `preview` from the newest row per conv, and `unread` as `COUNT(*) WHERE conv = ? AND d > <read:conv marker>`. This DELETES the `unread:` counter keys and `bump_unread` entirely — the count becomes derived from the messages, so it can no longer drift from them, which is the class of bug the backfill-conv-index script exists to repair.

TESTS FIRST, ws/src/__tests__/hub-do.test.ts: insert rows across two convs with a `read:` marker mid-stream; assert /msgs returns the newest 50 ascending; assert `before` excludes the boundary message; assert /convs returns one entry per conv ordered by `last` with correct unread and preview; assert a muted conv is excluded. AND assert `list_convs` issues exactly ONE `storage.list` call — spy on it. That spy is the permanent regression guard against the N+1 coming back. Watch them all fail first.
